{"kind":"report","text":"Milestone B advice (intermediate, not the final verdict). Inspected exact commit 8102fc0c371c22ad64076b007c7c51f8deac1861 and parent 7ffb64f4b0b8d440037186d6e0b633a6e8a3c173; go test and go vet pass on all four packages. The provenance correction was done right this time: the malformed request was withdrawn and the replacement cites the verbatim hash, which resolves.\n\nPARENT COMMIT 7ffb64f — the report #39 corrections are properly closed. Comma joins are refused in query shape (closing the confirmed ownership evasion, with a regression test), unquoted asterisks are rejected in JSONata source with correct quote/escape scanning (closing the wildcard nondeterminism and the multiplication-free interim honestly documented in a comment), the wall timeout is a named constant explicitly designated a safety net, RuntimeID is bumped to @2, tables now get the ambient-function check, JSON event columns are excluded from read parameters, and the unreachable 'missing' coverage state is gone. One side effect to accept knowingly: rejecting every unquoted asterisk also rejects JSONata /* block comments */; document that agent-authored programs cannot contain block comments, or scan comments separately.\n\nMILESTONE B INTAKE — the structure is right: single-writer SQLite with defensive config, positions assigned from a persistent delivery_head inside the same transaction as inserts and obligation capture (contiguous and monotonic even across drained content), capacity checked inside that transaction (atomic under the one-connection pool), settle-then-delete keeps a record exactly until no obligation is pending, consumer-less acceptance burns positions without retaining content, ErrFull maps to a retryable 503 with Retry-After, gzip is double-bounded, loopback is enforced by explicit IP, and unknown protojson fields are refused on the JSON path. Obligation settlement is idempotent for repeated same-state settles, which is what crash recovery between projection commit and obligation completion needs.\n\nBLOCKING CORRECTIONS before final integration:\n\nB1: sum/histogram temporality and monotonicity are lost. The approved spec requires the canonical record to preserve metric identity, temporality and monotonicity. flattenMetrics embeds only the data point as the otel copy (makeRecord receives the point message), and aggregation_temporality / is_monotonic live on the enclosing Sum/Histogram/ExponentialHistogram message, so they are preserved nowhere. A cumulative-vs-delta counter is semantically different for every rollup fold; add temporality (and is_monotonic for sums) to the canonical metric map.\n\nB2: the byte-stability milestone test is missing. Milestone B requires proving canonical JSON and flattening order are byte-stable. The current stability argument is subtle: protojson.Marshal output is deliberately randomized between runs, and stability is only restored because the output is laundered through json.Unmarshal into Go maps and re-marshaled with encoding/json's sorted keys. That laundering also coerces every JSON number in the otel copy through float64 (int64/uint64 survive only because protojson strings them). This deserves a pinned test: decode the same fixture twice (protobuf and JSON paths), assert identical canonical bytes and digests, and assert a 2^60-magnitude integer attribute survives exactly. Without the test, a protojson or encoding/json upgrade can silently change every content digest; note the canonicalization library set effectively belongs in RuntimeID.\n\nADVISORIES, non-blocking:\n\nA1: PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON is executed once in initialize, but it is per-connection state and the ON DELETE CASCADE cleanup depends on it; database/sql can replace its single connection after an error. Move foreign_keys (and trusted_schema) into the per-connection open callback where the Config calls already are.\n\nA2: settle() on an obligation whose record was already deleted returns an error (\"obligation not pending: no rows\"). After the projection layer lands, recovery replays completions; decide the contract now — either settle treats a missing obligation row as already-settled success (deletion only happens after every obligation left pending), or the recovery caller must swallow that error. The first is cleaner.\n\nA3: every Enqueue runs SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(canonical_bytes) over up to 100k rows before inserting — an O(n) scan per request under concurrency 16. Maintain running record/byte counters in inbox_state and adjust on insert/delete.\n\nA4: the request deadline context only covers the consumer snapshot and Enqueue; body read and decode are size-bounded but not time-bounded. Fine at this milestone, but set http.Server Read/Write timeouts when the engine assembles the server, or a stalled sender holds one of the 16 slots indefinitely.\n\nA5: duplicate attribute keys reject the whole request (refuse-rather-than-guess, and OTLP says keys SHOULD be unique). Consistent with the design; just ensure the eventual harness compatibility fixtures prove real Claude Code / Codex / OpenCode exports never trip it, since one duplicated key poisons an entire batch.\n\nA6: ValidateLoopbackAddress rejects the hostname form \"localhost\" (explicit IP required). Reasonable and stricter than the spec; state it in configuration docs so a copy-pasted localhost:4318 failure is self-explanatory.\n\nA7: log records with a zero observed_time and spans reuse start_time as the envelope time field; both are per spec, but the span canonical map carries name only in the envelope and status/events/links only inside the otel copy — completeness holds via otel, so this is fine; mention in the fold-authoring docs that span status lives at record.otel.status.\n\nProceed with projection work; fix B1 and B2 in this package before the final integration head. The provenance discipline this round was exactly right — keep citing rev-parse output verbatim."}